1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,340
MCP, the Model Context Protocol, was a standard proposed by Anthropik, but now adopted by many other companies and by many developers as a way to give MLM access to more context and to more tools.

2
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:23,700
There are a lot of developers developing around the MCP ecosystem, and so learning about this will give you a lot more access to resources for your applications.

3
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:24,800
Let's take a look.

4
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:29,200
This is the pain point that MCP attempts to solve.

5
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:38,800
If one developer is writing an application that wants to integrate with data from Slack in Google Drive and GitHub or access data from a Postgres database,

6
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:48,900
then they might write code to wrap around Slack APIs to have functions to provide to the application, write code to wrap around Google Drive APIs to post the application,

7
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:53,000
and similarly for these other tools or data sources.

8
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:59,020
Then what has been happening in the developer community is if a different team is building a different application,

9
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:04,540
then they too will integrate by themselves with Slack and Google Drive and GitHub and so on.

10
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:10,320
So many developers were all building custom wrappers around these types of data sources.

11
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:20,800
And so if there are M applications being developed and there are N tools out there, the total amount of work done by the community was M times N.

12
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:34,800
What MCP did was propose a standard for applications to get access to tools and data sources so that the total work that needs to be done by the community is now M plus N rather than M times N.

13
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:42,600
The initial design of MCP focused a lot on how to give more context to an LLM or how to fetch data.

14
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:47,520
So a lot of the initial tools were ones that would just fetch data.

15
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:52,280
And if you read the MCP documentation, that refers to these as resources.

16
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:59,200
But MCP gives access to both data as well as the more general functions that an application may want to call.

17
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:19,420
And it turns out that there are many MCP clients. These are the applications that want access to tools or to data as well as service, which are often the software wrappers that then give access to data in Slack or GitHub or Google Drive or allows you to take actions at these different types of resources.

18
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:29,180
So today, there's a rapidly growing list of MCP clients that consume the tools or the resources as well as MCP service that provide the tools and the resources.

19
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:37,820
And I hope that you find it useful to build your own MCP client. Your application maybe one day will be an MCP client.

20
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,340
And if you want to provide resources to other developers, maybe you can build your own MCP server someday.

21
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:54,180
Let me show you a quick example of using an MCP client. This is a cloud desktop app, and it has been connected to a GitHub MCP server.

22
00:02:54,180 --> 00:03:01,360
So when I enter this query, summarize the readme.markdown from the GitHub repo at this URL. This is actually an AI Suite repo.

23
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:08,280
Then this application, which is an MCP client, uses the GitHub MCP server with a request.

24
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:12,820
Please get the file readme.markdown from the repo AI Suite from this repo.

25
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:25,100
And then it gets this response, which is pretty long. All of this is then fed back to the LM as context. And the LM then generates the summary of the markdown file.

26
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:30,660
Now, let me enter another request, which is, let me enter, what are the latest pull requests?

27
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:38,740
This, in turn, causes the LM to use the MCP server to make a different request, to list the pull request.

28
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:46,380
This is another tool provided by GitHub's MCP server. And so it makes this request with repo AI Suite,

29
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:53,440
sort according to update it, list 20, and so on. And then it gets this response, which is fed back to the LM.

30
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:58,760
And the LM then you write this nice text summary of the latest pull requests for this repo.

31
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:05,320
MCP is an important standard. If you want to learn more about it, DeepLand.AI also has a short course

32
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:11,320
that goes much deeper into just the MCP protocol that you can check out after finishing this course,

33
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:17,060
if you're interested. But I hope this video gives you a brief overview of why it's useful,

34
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:21,220
and also why many developers are now building to this standard.

35
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:27,660
This brings us to the last video on tool use, and I hope that by giving you LM access to tools,

36
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,400
you're going to build agentic applications that are much more powerful.

37
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:38,400
In the next module, we'll talk about evaluations and error analysis.

38
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:44,640
It turns out that one of the things I've seen that distinguishes people that can execute agentic workflows

39
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:53,480
really well versus teams that are not as efficient at it is your ability to drive a disciplined evaluation process.

40
